{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fe58279ece5f64c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013 (FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack)\n10\t\n11\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold (two affected components — KServe and\n12\tPlatform Operator — and the certification work stream carries mandatory human\n13\treview-board gates and an external accredited laboratory, so ≥67% High AI\n14\timplementability does not hold); not documentation-only (image and CI code\n15\tchanges are required). Full decomposition follows.\n16\t\n17\t## Epic List\n18\t\n19\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n20\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of KServe serving images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 remediation of Platform Operator and serving data plane images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites documentation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P0 |\n25\t\n26\tNote on E004's priority: HLR-4 is P2, but the docs-authoring priority exception\n27\tderives the epic's priority from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical`\n28\t→ P0. This is rule-driven, not a priority inversion.\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 KServe image FIPS remediation (P0)\"]\n35\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator / data plane FIPS remediation (P0)\"]\n36\t    E003[\"E003 Compliance evidence package and submission (P0)\"]\n37\t    E004[\"E004 FIPS posture documentation (P0, docs-authoring)\"]\n38\t    E001 --> E003\n39\t    E002 --> E003\n40\t    E001 --> E004\n41\t    E002 --> E004\n42\t    E003 --> E004\n43\t```\n44\t\n45\tCritical path: E001 → E003 → E004 (length 3).\n46\t\n47\t## DAG Justification\n48\t\n49\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n50\t|------|------|-----------|\n51\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 + Step 5 shared-surface division | Different components, different repos, no shared artifacts → parallel. The HLR-3 FIPS-regression CI deliverable applies across the same builds being remediated, so it is divided per surface rather than extracted into a standalone CI epic: each remediation epic delivers and verifies the gating check for its own component's builds. A standalone enforcement epic would carve one logical change into a work-type epic and leave each remediation epic's slice incomplete. |\n52\t| E001 → E003 | Rules 12/13 (producer → consumer) | The evidence package is assembled from the remediation epics' primary engineering deliverables — the per-image module inventory, the build attestations, and the FIPS-enabled-cluster test results — and documents the remediated state, so it cannot truthfully be assembled before remediation completes. This is not the Rule 13 configuration-reference carve-out (image digests or build-pipeline side-effect attestations filled in at execution time): the consumed items here are the producing epic's core deliverables, and the security review board signs off on remediation evidence at its milestones. |\n53\t| E002 → E003 | Rules 12/13 (producer → consumer) | Same rationale as E001 → E003, for the Platform Operator and serving data plane images. |\n54\t| E001 → E004, E002 → E004, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | The documentation epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy: docs describe what was built, including the FIPS support posture that only exists once remediation and evidence work land. |\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n57\t\n58\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n59\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n60\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002 | Split by component per the epic boundary rules: E001 covers the KServe component images; E002 covers the Platform Operator and serving data plane images. Together they cover \"all serving-stack images\". |\n61\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003 | Evidence package (module inventory, build attestations, test results) assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab. |\n62\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 (bundled), E002 (bundled) | The gating CI check runs over the same images, builds, and CI configuration surface that the P0 remediation converts — a shared surface, not a deferrable feature. Per the shared-surface enforcement rule the deliverable is divided per surface: the epic that converts a surface also authors and verifies the FIPS regression gate for that surface, so each remediation epic's HLR Traceability lists HLR-3 as bundled. Not independently deferrable: a standalone CI epic would build enforcement for surfaces another epic is still converting, leave each remediation epic's slice incomplete, and force a second pass over the same build configuration. |\n63\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | New documentation topics (FIPS support posture, cluster prerequisites) are substantive content authoring — a distinct work-product stream → separate docs-authoring epic per the work-product boundary rule. |\n64\t\n65\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n66\t\n67\t## Health Warnings\n68\t\n69\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** The strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no per-component change/owner detail, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no NFRs. Teams were assigned by inference: KServe → RHOAI Serving (from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`, \"Owning team: RHOAI Serving\"); Platform Operator → RHOAI Platform; evidence package → RHOAI Serving (owning team of the primary serving component); documentation → RHOAI Documentation. Confirm all four with the PM before scheduling.\n70\t2. **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file.** Only KServe has a context file under `.context/architecture-context/`. The strategy's premise is that Platform Operator already ships serving-stack images (it links non-validated crypto \"today\"), so it was treated as an existing component rather than generating a repo-onboarding chain — onboarding a new midstream repo would contradict the strategy's own problem statement. Consequence: `architecture_claims` is scored 0 for E002 and the component's repo/image inventory should be verified with its owning team.\n71\t3. **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity.** The strategy attributes the serving data plane to Platform Operator (the only two Affected Components are KServe and Platform Operator). If Serving Runtimes images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit — KServe's model-execution dependencies per the architecture context) are actually meant, E002's scope needs adjustment or a separate strategy; they are not in the declared scope here.\n72\t4. **External schedule gates are risks, not ACs.** Certification-lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and review-board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are recorded as Risks/Notes on E003; E003's acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion point the team controls — package assembled, board milestones passed, package submitted to the lab and triaged. Certification outcome itself is not an in-scope deliverable.\n73\t5. **HLR-3 (P1) is bundled into P0 epics.** Deliberate, per the shared-surface enforcement rule — see the HLR-3 matrix row for the full rationale.\n74\t6. **No Investigation epics.** The strategy states \"None on scope\" for open questions and contains no conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks table. The crypto-usage audit (which images link non-validated crypto libraries) does not change which epics exist or what they do — remediation proceeds the same way regardless, and a failed audit finding is fix-and-retry within the same epic — so it is captured as an acceptance criterion (the per-image module inventory) on E001 and E002 rather than a separate Investigation.\n75\t\n76\t## Tiered Delivery\n77\t\n78\tNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is the\n79\taccredited certification laboratory, and HLR-2's deliverable ends at submission\n80\tthrough the security review board — the certification outcome sits outside the\n81\tHLRs. All in-scope work (remediation, CI enforcement, evidence assembly,\n82\tdocumentation) proceeds without tiering; the lab gate lies after the last\n83\tin-scope deliverable.\n84\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ab1609e95ab4f690","agent_id":"a4e1c6bbe89212fca","session_id":"1394d656-32ae-4405-935b-3f7c1b336014","uuid":"8ce8ddcd-f87e-43ab-998d-9d01b9a56f79","timestamp":"2026-10-26T13:23:16.451Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9fe58279ece5f64c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
